✅ 1. Types of Deployment Strategies (CI/CD Deployment Strategies)
Explain each with use cases + pros/cons (must-know for 5 yrs experience).
1. Rolling Deployment
How it works:
Gradually replace old versions with new versions instance-by-instance.
No downtime.
Use case:
Microservices on Kubernetes, EC2 Auto Scaling Groups.
Advantages:
Zero downtime
Easy rollback (if using multiple replicas)
Disadvantages:
Hard to test full new version until rollout completes

2. Blue-Green Deployment
How it works:
Two identical environments: Blue (old) and Green (new)
Traffic is switched from Blue → Green after testing.
Use case:
Critical applications where downtime must be zero
Load balancer (ALB/NLB) or Route53 weighted routing
Advantages:
Instant rollback
True zero downtime
Disadvantages:
Double infrastructure cost

3. Canary Deployment
How it works:
Release new version to a small group of users (5–10%)
Monitor performance
Gradually increase traffic
Use case:
High-risk changes
Kubernetes + Istio or Argo Rollouts
Advantages:
Very safe, issues found early
Dynamic traffic control
Disadvantages:
More complex monitoring & routing setup

4. A/B Testing Deployment
How it works:
Route different versions to different audiences based on rules
Used to compare features or UI changes
Use case:
Feature validation
Marketing experiments

5. Recreate Deployment
How it works:
Stop old version → Deploy new version
Causes temporary downtime
Use case:
Non-critical apps
Apps not supporting multiple versions at same time

6. Shadow Deployment
How it works:
Duplicate traffic is sent silently to new version
Old version handles real requests
Use case:
ML model replacements
Risk-free comparison of outputs

✅ 2. Workflow of Deployment (CI/CD Pipeline Workflow)
Here is a standard end-to-end deployment workflow expected from a DevOps engineer:

Step 1 — Code Commit (Developer Stage)
Developers push code into Git (GitHub/GitLab/Bitbucket)
A Pull Request is created
Static checks run (linting, style check)

Step 2 — CI Pipeline (Build + Test Stage)
